[[:db/retract 17592186168711 :answer-field/choices 17592186168712] [:db/retract 17592186168711 :answer-field/choices 17592186168713] [:db/retract 17592186168711 :answer-field/choices 17592186168714] [:db/retract 17592186168711 :answer-field/choices 17592186168715] [:db/retract 17592186168711 :answer-field/choices 17592186168716] {:db/id 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dccc7f7f-0cab-5758-b1da-5b22069d8a23" "question-ec31d0f0-f633-5965-938b-954592f5e7bc" "question-8639ec93-c7f5-5ea1-8df7-3159f8c54b2d" "question-fec27738-a1e3-5883-83b7-8672ecd1a476"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0f963f5f-3418-5f15-a044-1af0d45a6d3b" "question-ef2a7e7d-9054-51ec-bb29-4e186ac65a15" "question-621adbef-22f1-5cf5-b77b-1bf6fb7f602e" "question-5645d046-8b12-5960-a3e0-e0429413dd27"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-380026fe-2d4e-5527-8902-7fe90302976f" "question-9d4f7d2e-5a54-5483-93ff-febe106e9483" "question-44ac1bd4-9843-582f-80a5-1b691b1d5da4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172308 :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+1,\\,{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/c3/c3c617522488e6ebf47dd2b2a52c43a1fe06ccaef8cd1e8752ab4df5e2d4bd64.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+1$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168711 :answer-field/correct "answer-9d5ab6e6-3ed2-5325-ad32-339384996043" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d5ab6e6-3ed2-5325-ad32-339384996043" "answer-ae24f312-5321-5cc9-87dd-f8625d1a40c3" "answer-e838f00f-7aa2-51c0-8734-9a7dc0bd6c20" "answer-b7765c6a-5ae2-501d-ad46-c25d34ebcac5" "answer-6730175f-e2ca-5f18-8300-20f526084f78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d5ab6e6-3ed2-5325-ad32-339384996043" :answer/id #uuid "9d5ab6e6-3ed2-5325-ad32-339384996043" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(x+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae24f312-5321-5cc9-87dd-f8625d1a40c3" :answer/id #uuid "ae24f312-5321-5cc9-87dd-f8625d1a40c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(x-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e838f00f-7aa2-51c0-8734-9a7dc0bd6c20" :answer/id #uuid "e838f00f-7aa2-51c0-8734-9a7dc0bd6c20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(y-x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7765c6a-5ae2-501d-ad46-c25d34ebcac5" :answer/id #uuid "b7765c6a-5ae2-501d-ad46-c25d34ebcac5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6730175f-e2ca-5f18-8300-20f526084f78" :answer/id #uuid "6730175f-e2ca-5f18-8300-20f526084f78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dccc7f7f-0cab-5758-b1da-5b22069d8a23" :question/id #uuid "dccc7f7f-0cab-5758-b1da-5b22069d8a23" :question/math-academy-id "q-127203" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨2+x{y}^{2},\\,3-{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/0b/0b79306d0e78da924aa31b3bf1c73b7430ca322abc7f5a9b3107bb0a4d662182.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2+x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3-{x}^{2}y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a624492-2c9c-5aeb-9ae8-4412b203d919"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a624492-2c9c-5aeb-9ae8-4412b203d919" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a624492-2c9c-5aeb-9ae8-4412b203d919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-69428f30-71a1-5d98-bab5-a1556421d756" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7043ade4-6766-5180-8bd2-76e0d272838a" "answer-c1dcad37-cd08-51d7-ba24-ddf20a23ed67" "answer-e12e0d19-bdc2-57b6-9364-8156f4f5ea08" "answer-4c4895c8-33dd-51f2-b0a9-62d7e0bb7e4a" "answer-69428f30-71a1-5d98-bab5-a1556421d756"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7043ade4-6766-5180-8bd2-76e0d272838a" :answer/id #uuid "7043ade4-6766-5180-8bd2-76e0d272838a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1dcad37-cd08-51d7-ba24-ddf20a23ed67" :answer/id #uuid "c1dcad37-cd08-51d7-ba24-ddf20a23ed67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12e0d19-bdc2-57b6-9364-8156f4f5ea08" :answer/id #uuid "e12e0d19-bdc2-57b6-9364-8156f4f5ea08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c4895c8-33dd-51f2-b0a9-62d7e0bb7e4a" :answer/id #uuid "4c4895c8-33dd-51f2-b0a9-62d7e0bb7e4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69428f30-71a1-5d98-bab5-a1556421d756" :answer/id #uuid "69428f30-71a1-5d98-bab5-a1556421d756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec31d0f0-f633-5965-938b-954592f5e7bc" :question/id #uuid "ec31d0f0-f633-5965-938b-954592f5e7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127226" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{4}+{y}^{3},\\,({x}^{3}+5)y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(2,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/63/636bfd79297e6fef4b0e56af9b0cb22f2e9a8061e833ea1dcf07e7958b870cdc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{4}+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}+5)y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{x}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & ={x}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5({x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=5({x}^{3}+1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3f093a1f-fd70-5b60-bb5c-a3a70624352c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3f093a1f-fd70-5b60-bb5c-a3a70624352c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f093a1f-fd70-5b60-bb5c-a3a70624352c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6929e9f0-853a-5eae-90fd-c61e9acb4821" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fad12fdf-85ee-5681-8fee-a7e21e160d4b" "answer-be2b7276-0419-561c-a09e-7c016dde655a" "answer-6929e9f0-853a-5eae-90fd-c61e9acb4821" "answer-8581b009-276f-5642-a422-5b78da828fa6" "answer-89bd431e-528a-55dc-b640-1fbdebadacfe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fad12fdf-85ee-5681-8fee-a7e21e160d4b" :answer/id #uuid "fad12fdf-85ee-5681-8fee-a7e21e160d4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2({x}^{3}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be2b7276-0419-561c-a09e-7c016dde655a" :answer/id #uuid "be2b7276-0419-561c-a09e-7c016dde655a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6929e9f0-853a-5eae-90fd-c61e9acb4821" :answer/id #uuid "6929e9f0-853a-5eae-90fd-c61e9acb4821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5({x}^{3}+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8581b009-276f-5642-a422-5b78da828fa6" :answer/id #uuid "8581b009-276f-5642-a422-5b78da828fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{x}^{3}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89bd431e-528a-55dc-b640-1fbdebadacfe" :answer/id #uuid "89bd431e-528a-55dc-b640-1fbdebadacfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8639ec93-c7f5-5ea1-8df7-3159f8c54b2d" :question/id #uuid "8639ec93-c7f5-5ea1-8df7-3159f8c54b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127232" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,-1),(1,-1),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(6xy+y{x}^{2})\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/86/861b873005c03ea6c25255f2febc975ff153d939780ec46e4f4503cbb50640dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6xy+y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=6y+2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6y+2xy)+(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17417f10-d5b1-59a3-bbd0-62b09d7aaa49"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17417f10-d5b1-59a3-bbd0-62b09d7aaa49" :answer-field/id #uuid "17417f10-d5b1-59a3-bbd0-62b09d7aaa49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2153ce06-d6d5-5127-8a6c-97309bd972d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85eceb92-6af4-585c-adf1-d11f60bbf317" "answer-6fe0b5de-0918-5eb2-8f3c-3bb3e4a8b13f" "answer-29c7cf32-a75b-5b72-bb10-1df951e308a9" "answer-c4de078e-efd8-5984-b3ca-2569a1db93a5" "answer-2153ce06-d6d5-5127-8a6c-97309bd972d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85eceb92-6af4-585c-adf1-d11f60bbf317" :answer/id #uuid "85eceb92-6af4-585c-adf1-d11f60bbf317" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fe0b5de-0918-5eb2-8f3c-3bb3e4a8b13f" :answer/id #uuid "6fe0b5de-0918-5eb2-8f3c-3bb3e4a8b13f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29c7cf32-a75b-5b72-bb10-1df951e308a9" :answer/id #uuid "29c7cf32-a75b-5b72-bb10-1df951e308a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4de078e-efd8-5984-b3ca-2569a1db93a5" :answer/id #uuid "c4de078e-efd8-5984-b3ca-2569a1db93a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2x(2+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2153ce06-d6d5-5127-8a6c-97309bd972d8" :answer/id #uuid "2153ce06-d6d5-5127-8a6c-97309bd972d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fec27738-a1e3-5883-83b7-8672ecd1a476" :question/id #uuid "fec27738-a1e3-5883-83b7-8672ecd1a476" :question/math-academy-id "q-127224" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(0,-2),$ and $(1,-1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9472021d3973167a7601785803837627476a4e8b1460e42f87e91ecb99d7e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8f6f773-72e6-553b-a49d-36c27b57974f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8f6f773-72e6-553b-a49d-36c27b57974f" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f6f773-72e6-553b-a49d-36c27b57974f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8cceeb19-30be-531c-aaa0-0ea7e33dd634" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8cceeb19-30be-531c-aaa0-0ea7e33dd634" "answer-5c52dcea-70d7-560b-8792-445eff50c6a9" "answer-209d3320-50d9-50ef-9a25-a823b828f588" "answer-eaf9c71a-e752-5728-b94c-b39be38cdb47" "answer-3eed97bf-f19a-5ae8-9a9c-33659aaf6987"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cceeb19-30be-531c-aaa0-0ea7e33dd634" :answer/id #uuid "8cceeb19-30be-531c-aaa0-0ea7e33dd634" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+3{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c52dcea-70d7-560b-8792-445eff50c6a9" :answer/id #uuid "5c52dcea-70d7-560b-8792-445eff50c6a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-209d3320-50d9-50ef-9a25-a823b828f588" :answer/id #uuid "209d3320-50d9-50ef-9a25-a823b828f588" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eaf9c71a-e752-5728-b94c-b39be38cdb47" :answer/id #uuid "eaf9c71a-e752-5728-b94c-b39be38cdb47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eed97bf-f19a-5ae8-9a9c-33659aaf6987" :answer/id #uuid "3eed97bf-f19a-5ae8-9a9c-33659aaf6987" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172307 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/37/3786d0a5fb99d1a32d761e067f9c49b6b7da05a11fc555dd94a4ef42d753f129.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x-{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-1)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168705 :answer-field/correct 17592186168706 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f963f5f-3418-5f15-a044-1af0d45a6d3b" :question/id #uuid "0f963f5f-3418-5f15-a044-1af0d45a6d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127305" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-4xy,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e0bf7b88eadd199edc1997410b7ddbaa74b204e7d0f8afef7c9b17c28d8f8dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{x}^{2}-4yx]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[({1}^{2}-4y(1))-((-1)^{2}-4y(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-8y\\,dy \\\\  & =-4{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-4(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68e0080e-3bc6-5b4f-ad81-a8b266b3aa68"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68e0080e-3bc6-5b4f-ad81-a8b266b3aa68" :answer-field/id #uuid "68e0080e-3bc6-5b4f-ad81-a8b266b3aa68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e38494e3-564d-5089-a5ec-aa68869b4e33" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4ef2656-ec3b-5442-90ba-703850ba3398" "answer-a888cc31-d112-55bf-8f2c-cc635b42c57e" "answer-072b4d5b-a518-50b1-9fd5-372915316f5c" "answer-e38494e3-564d-5089-a5ec-aa68869b4e33" "answer-231a4e7e-143a-55ee-96ec-349dc902c4d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4ef2656-ec3b-5442-90ba-703850ba3398" :answer/id #uuid "e4ef2656-ec3b-5442-90ba-703850ba3398" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a888cc31-d112-55bf-8f2c-cc635b42c57e" :answer/id #uuid "a888cc31-d112-55bf-8f2c-cc635b42c57e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-072b4d5b-a518-50b1-9fd5-372915316f5c" :answer/id #uuid "072b4d5b-a518-50b1-9fd5-372915316f5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e38494e3-564d-5089-a5ec-aa68869b4e33" :answer/id #uuid "e38494e3-564d-5089-a5ec-aa68869b4e33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-231a4e7e-143a-55ee-96ec-349dc902c4d0" :answer/id #uuid "231a4e7e-143a-55ee-96ec-349dc902c4d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef2a7e7d-9054-51ec-bb29-4e186ac65a15" :question/id #uuid "ef2a7e7d-9054-51ec-bb29-4e186ac65a15" :question/math-academy-id "q-127307" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\,{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb0033d2b275442ee92cc0b805c71102641c670ea1b0edba97d31a9262795c83.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,x\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\cdot 1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2{y}^{4}+2{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}+2(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd4aac36-d301-5889-827c-4b73f4384b55"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd4aac36-d301-5889-827c-4b73f4384b55" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4aac36-d301-5889-827c-4b73f4384b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7dd7d3e5-183d-50b2-bbd9-75b14c583ab6" "answer-974a14ee-cedf-5300-8d94-d8f4a66e99c1" "answer-fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" "answer-799a4193-8f0b-5d08-a4bf-2ba532ac8bb9" "answer-60eea807-02ce-5996-b952-62ec842109d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dd7d3e5-183d-50b2-bbd9-75b14c583ab6" :answer/id #uuid "7dd7d3e5-183d-50b2-bbd9-75b14c583ab6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-974a14ee-cedf-5300-8d94-d8f4a66e99c1" :answer/id #uuid "974a14ee-cedf-5300-8d94-d8f4a66e99c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer/id #uuid "fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-799a4193-8f0b-5d08-a4bf-2ba532ac8bb9" :answer/id #uuid "799a4193-8f0b-5d08-a4bf-2ba532ac8bb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60eea807-02ce-5996-b952-62ec842109d3" :answer/id #uuid "60eea807-02ce-5996-b952-62ec842109d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-621adbef-22f1-5cf5-b77b-1bf6fb7f602e" :question/id #uuid "621adbef-22f1-5cf5-b77b-1bf6fb7f602e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127326" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{y}-4xy,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7400787e09600d370fa5c554e616fad6ba3a126a09725cfb236502f81a7e70e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4xy-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(-4(1)^{2}y-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4y-6{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[-2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3})-(-2(-1)^{2}-2(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-501779b1-729b-5f0b-8f00-ddfd19deba04"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-501779b1-729b-5f0b-8f00-ddfd19deba04" :answer-field/id #uuid "501779b1-729b-5f0b-8f00-ddfd19deba04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac21a517-9ce2-51e1-a210-d1342e658153" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84832c07-1cf7-5890-95f5-93379bbcf9e7" "answer-ac21a517-9ce2-51e1-a210-d1342e658153" "answer-a513c7fb-0fe0-56ce-955a-00f68e2e1226" "answer-eb98dc31-4f54-5896-a0d6-e621b6d1e1f7" "answer-036be383-69ad-5c54-97f4-245cc64ebdc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84832c07-1cf7-5890-95f5-93379bbcf9e7" :answer/id #uuid "84832c07-1cf7-5890-95f5-93379bbcf9e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac21a517-9ce2-51e1-a210-d1342e658153" :answer/id #uuid "ac21a517-9ce2-51e1-a210-d1342e658153" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a513c7fb-0fe0-56ce-955a-00f68e2e1226" :answer/id #uuid "a513c7fb-0fe0-56ce-955a-00f68e2e1226" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb98dc31-4f54-5896-a0d6-e621b6d1e1f7" :answer/id #uuid "eb98dc31-4f54-5896-a0d6-e621b6d1e1f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-036be383-69ad-5c54-97f4-245cc64ebdc5" :answer/id #uuid "036be383-69ad-5c54-97f4-245cc64ebdc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5645d046-8b12-5960-a3e0-e0429413dd27" :question/id #uuid "5645d046-8b12-5960-a3e0-e0429413dd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-127337" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\,{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b25d12781632bcc03576a79e7fbf1b22806fc68e3f521ecd602f1b0732d8d90d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{7}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[0-((-1)^{2}y-(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-y-1)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-(1))-(-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15f3b20c-fcac-5124-a093-6cfd6cef919f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15f3b20c-fcac-5124-a093-6cfd6cef919f" :answer-field/id #uuid "15f3b20c-fcac-5124-a093-6cfd6cef919f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db270bba-95df-51f5-b9d1-ccab96038132" "answer-783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" "answer-9ceb053a-ae1f-53f2-9912-d9d9157552fa" "answer-5ebdbb3a-9cd8-57c7-9768-3437a54bb5b2" "answer-1afc0e4b-7ec3-5e04-a320-b09bbb7450d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db270bba-95df-51f5-b9d1-ccab96038132" :answer/id #uuid "db270bba-95df-51f5-b9d1-ccab96038132" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer/id #uuid "783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ceb053a-ae1f-53f2-9912-d9d9157552fa" :answer/id #uuid "9ceb053a-ae1f-53f2-9912-d9d9157552fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ebdbb3a-9cd8-57c7-9768-3437a54bb5b2" :answer/id #uuid "5ebdbb3a-9cd8-57c7-9768-3437a54bb5b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1afc0e4b-7ec3-5e04-a320-b09bbb7450d5" :answer/id #uuid "1afc0e4b-7ec3-5e04-a320-b09bbb7450d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172305 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+2y,\\,{x}^{2}+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,{|}_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={1}^{4}-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168693 :answer-field/correct 17592186168696 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-380026fe-2d4e-5527-8902-7fe90302976f" :question/id #uuid "380026fe-2d4e-5527-8902-7fe90302976f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127302" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨14xy,\\,6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=14xy$ and $Q(x,y)=6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{y}^{2}+6{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{x}^{6}+6{x}^{5})\\,dx \\\\  & =({x}^{7}+{x}^{6})\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f5abcf2-857d-5997-8843-79c2143f0e91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f5abcf2-857d-5997-8843-79c2143f0e91" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5abcf2-857d-5997-8843-79c2143f0e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be728807-7dfc-57ad-844a-74242eaae230" :answer-field/choices ["answer-be728807-7dfc-57ad-844a-74242eaae230" "answer-f65df826-8fea-532c-b10f-fd1044e7f4a0" "answer-9e8a5a99-44bd-5b11-83dd-6c16e9e4e40b" "answer-f1954043-6aaa-5ee4-807d-74a521f1268a" "answer-a581e533-3d41-5f80-8a3e-7a4d97fd2b2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be728807-7dfc-57ad-844a-74242eaae230" :answer/id #uuid "be728807-7dfc-57ad-844a-74242eaae230" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f65df826-8fea-532c-b10f-fd1044e7f4a0" :answer/id #uuid "f65df826-8fea-532c-b10f-fd1044e7f4a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e8a5a99-44bd-5b11-83dd-6c16e9e4e40b" :answer/id #uuid "9e8a5a99-44bd-5b11-83dd-6c16e9e4e40b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1954043-6aaa-5ee4-807d-74a521f1268a" :answer/id #uuid "f1954043-6aaa-5ee4-807d-74a521f1268a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a581e533-3d41-5f80-8a3e-7a4d97fd2b2b" :answer/id #uuid "a581e533-3d41-5f80-8a3e-7a4d97fd2b2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172304 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨3{x}^{5}+2y,\\,3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/269e3c7f92cee66c78f3226e7c967eb51153503c598d2e4d1e4b56a33e646ac4.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3{x}^{5}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=15{x}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{5}+6xy]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(3{y}^{5}+6{y}^{2})-(-3{y}^{5}-6{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{y}^{5}+12{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{6}+4{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+4)-0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168687 :answer-field/correct 17592186168689 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d4f7d2e-5a54-5483-93ff-febe106e9483" :question/id #uuid "9d4f7d2e-5a54-5483-93ff-febe106e9483" :question/math-academy-id "q-127370" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨-x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y+1,$ $\\,x={e}^{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/72/728036ffc90e2476017b3102b6279f9c5900f0e7fc58536e76c9aa038eb04f02.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y+1\\le x\\le {e}^{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}+y-1)\\,dy \\\\  & =-{[{e}^{y}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[(e+\\frac{1}{2}-1)-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d52f83e4-8134-5921-a35f-e8152b063a24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d52f83e4-8134-5921-a35f-e8152b063a24" :answer-field/id #uuid "d52f83e4-8134-5921-a35f-e8152b063a24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e6d825d3-bc21-53d9-9b80-37d93f21f53c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-491c7d85-23b8-5360-a8a5-150039e21ba9" "answer-091bc970-fa77-5e71-85b9-f1ab8c6a0482" "answer-55384a96-bb48-572a-9f44-39ec987e63ac" "answer-e6d825d3-bc21-53d9-9b80-37d93f21f53c" "answer-c4d61b2f-57fc-5cda-b0b0-9b59235e0fb3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-491c7d85-23b8-5360-a8a5-150039e21ba9" :answer/id #uuid "491c7d85-23b8-5360-a8a5-150039e21ba9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-091bc970-fa77-5e71-85b9-f1ab8c6a0482" :answer/id #uuid "091bc970-fa77-5e71-85b9-f1ab8c6a0482" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55384a96-bb48-572a-9f44-39ec987e63ac" :answer/id #uuid "55384a96-bb48-572a-9f44-39ec987e63ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6d825d3-bc21-53d9-9b80-37d93f21f53c" :answer/id #uuid "e6d825d3-bc21-53d9-9b80-37d93f21f53c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4d61b2f-57fc-5cda-b0b0-9b59235e0fb3" :answer/id #uuid "c4d61b2f-57fc-5cda-b0b0-9b59235e0fb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+2e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-44ac1bd4-9843-582f-80a5-1b691b1d5da4" :question/id #uuid "44ac1bd4-9843-582f-80a5-1b691b1d5da4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127328" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+y,\\,xy+1⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y,$ $\\,x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2ecabe22395006f0c7ef0a3f914087fa324f4d59c7d7886c18907f824744643e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19474be3-1fd4-58b1-9c1c-f483db0b1748"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19474be3-1fd4-58b1-9c1c-f483db0b1748" :answer-field/id #uuid "19474be3-1fd4-58b1-9c1c-f483db0b1748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dbf01d52-6bde-5d5c-8f28-24beb09eed98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3452acaf-89b6-53ae-861d-c8309d517e98" "answer-4b779ef3-0c88-57d7-943a-271c2a83d88c" "answer-38444180-4e68-5f1d-94e7-63374144fcf9" "answer-395d167e-4282-5829-b24d-6655ea33a853" "answer-dbf01d52-6bde-5d5c-8f28-24beb09eed98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3452acaf-89b6-53ae-861d-c8309d517e98" :answer/id #uuid "3452acaf-89b6-53ae-861d-c8309d517e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b779ef3-0c88-57d7-943a-271c2a83d88c" :answer/id #uuid "4b779ef3-0c88-57d7-943a-271c2a83d88c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38444180-4e68-5f1d-94e7-63374144fcf9" :answer/id #uuid "38444180-4e68-5f1d-94e7-63374144fcf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-395d167e-4282-5829-b24d-6655ea33a853" :answer/id #uuid "395d167e-4282-5829-b24d-6655ea33a853" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbf01d52-6bde-5d5c-8f28-24beb09eed98" :answer/id #uuid "dbf01d52-6bde-5d5c-8f28-24beb09eed98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173207 :tutorial/content "There is some nice intuition behind the flux form of Green's theorem that makes it easier to remember.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P$ and $Q$ defined over a region $D.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf F$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/47/47a81125069be94b4ccda6f228ff1b8253f99595c6f3c4ae0ca710bdb796215f.png)\n\n    \n\nRecall that the divergence (i.e., the microscopic flux) of $\\mathbf F$ at a point is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the divergence at every point over the entire domain $D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us one side of Green's theorem in flux form.\n\nThe macroscopic divergence is simply the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the divergences with the flux, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux form of Green's theorem can be summarized in a single picture, as follows:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8ba4b7e853e3b59e8677b0954c3cfae1b9c482750e13a742bd4d45eda8005d.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172181 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
